pipeline were they involved in it you  can see from the from the graphic here  these represent individual companies and  what they're doing all the way from you  know developing and selling consumables  and arrays just you know producing  sequencing instruments and supplying  those conducting the sequencing aligning  and annotate and annotating the  sequences that were being done  interpretation and reporting and data  storage and bundling and this is again  not an exhaustive list that these are  the kinds of things that these various  companies are engaged in and what  they're actually doing is going to  impact what their motivation is whether  they have access to data or whether they  can share data and what what impacts  sort of different legal regulatory  issues have on them as a company so I  think where we're at now is really  trying to figure out as a community how  do we incentivize data sharing and I  feel really strongly that although we  may agree that as a scientific community  it is a moral imperative  if we're going to offer whole genome and  whole exome sequencing to patients in a  clinical context that we have robust  enough databases to be able to give them  answers to what we find and so there may  be a moral imperative to share data  broadly speaking and we can we can argue  about that but let's let's just assume  that but even if it is a moral  imperative I don't think that's enough  it can't just be a moral imperative so  many people have pointed out that we  need a business imperative for change  and innovation based on the needs of our  customers and that the barriers to  sharing a really a lack of enabling  infrastructure data governance uniform  policies appropriately constrained  standards and economic incentives so we  need to be thinking about this not just  from an ethical moral perspective but  also from a business perspective so this  leads me back to our policy Delphi that  we did and rounds three and four of that  of that project we 