al  program or somebody who's in academia or  we had also people who were in the tech  industry who were doing this from from  that perspective and having them all sit  in a room together and be able to say  okay well this is what's important to us  but this is what's important to you guys  and can how can we work together to try  to think through the businesses  incentives as well as the ethical  imperative to share data and create this  medical information comments and so the  goal of our project is to engage expert  stakeholders to inform polities policy  decisions about effective governance for  data sharing while using deliberative  methods to obtain informed public input  to make sure that the values rights and  interests of individuals whose data  populate the information Commons are  represented so we're doing that in three  stages we're currently working on a  landscape analysis really trying to get  a handle on what are the different  initiatives that are out there in these  different spaces and how are they  sharing data what models are they using  and what are they currently doing and  then we're going to be interviewing  experts stakeholders including members  of our advisory panel but as well as as  other individuals to try to get a sense  of how well identified models of what's  currently going on address key policy  issues and to explore innovative ideas  for addressing unresolved issues and  then finally we're going to take sort of  a couple of different models that are  that are being developed in terms of how  we create this medical information  Commons to members of the public through  citizens panels and this is really an  in-depth way of exploring the values and  soliciting public in  about these key policy issues and the  ways to address innovative innovative  problems and so we'll be doing citizens  panels across the United States to try  to get this really really in-depth  informed public input on some of these  issues so we're really really excited  about this project this is some very  preliminary data based on the first 22  initiatives that we've done extensive  data collection on and this is from a  publicly available data on the internet  but you can see the kinds of data that  are being banked it's not just genetic  data anymore it's not just sequence data  anymore its data its proteins and  phenotypes images demographics other  kinds of information so this gets back  to the issue of linking data and you can  see the kind of data sharing models that  are being adopted so of the 22 that we  initially looked at about thirty two  percent of them have the centralized  model 60 point four percent used a  centralized hub and about five percent  are currently using a federated model  and there may be other models out there  that we need to explore as well that  that offer alternative types of  structures so I just want to kind of I'm  getting close to ending but I want to  point out what participants perspectives  are on this and what we know about that  from our own work so this is from a  study that was done actually several  years ago but we asked individuals who  are being recruited into genomic studies  and these were primarily patients who  were sick themselves or parents of  pediatric patients who had sick kids and  they were asked to never being asked to  participate in genomic studies have  their biological specimens stored and  used for genomic analysis and then to  have their data shared and we did a  randomized trial of different types of  consents that gave varying amounts of  control over the decision to share their  data and the the different options we  gave them is whether their data can be  shared in publicly accessible databases  or open access databases versus shared  in these controlled access databases  where you have to go through like a data  access committee to get the data or not  sure at all and then at the end of the  study we sort of told everybody we  debrief them and said 