heir  privacy it was really about feeling  respected as a participant in the  research and so they felt like that was  a really important aspect of respect for  persons so in our in our new study that  we're doing on building a medical  information Commons we're going to try  to take the this sort of survey  interview data that we have from actual  research participants and try to explore  the values of the public under  buying data sharing and so we're going  to do this public deliberation and we're  going to look at public views on several  of the ethical issues that come up as  well as try to get at the value tensions  that are at stake when we talk about  sharing data and building a medical  information commons and i just want to  end by pointing out that i think one of  the major big elephants in the room when  we talk about data sharing when we talk  about data repositories and medical  information commons is this question of  who owns your genetic data and this is a  photo I don't know how many of you can  see this well but it's a it's a  photograph I found on the Internet of an  elephant on President Obama's back and  the reason I include this picture of  President Obama is because a couple  months ago he held a conference in the  white house around the precision  medicine initiative and in the  conference he was sort of coded as  saying I would like to think that if  somebody does a test on me or my genes  that that's mine but that's not always  how we define these issues so this  really brings up the question of who  owns your genetic data and what do we  mean by ownership I think what lawyers  mean by ownership might be very very  different than what patients and  participants think of when they say it's  my data or I own the data and what  rights and obligations do those various  meanings give rise to and I think that's  one of the critical issues that we need  to address as we start moving towards  building a medical information comments  so i will end 