 the science it would  benefit researchers and it would prevent  sort of the privatization of the genome  so the Bermuda principles have really  sort of formed a framework or foundation  for how we think about data sharing in  the genomics world but this slide really  just shows that we're not in Bermuda  anymore things have gotten a lot more  complicated and I don't want to  underestimate how complicated it was and  how difficult it was at the time to come  to an agreement and develop the Bermuda  because it was really challenging the  ways in which science was typically done  and it was really sort of paradigm  shifting in terms of of the culture and  the values of making data broadly  available and who gets credit and those  sorts of things but if it was hard back  in 1996 when we when we had the Bermuda  principles written it's exponentially  more difficult today and I'm going to  give you a couple of reasons of why I  think it's become so much more difficult  the first is that back when we had we  may wrote the Bermuda principles there  were five countries involved in the  human genome project China was added in  1999 but you could basically get 50  people in a room and they were sort of  all the most of the people that were  going to be impacted by the policy that  was developed so they could all give  their input and be part of the process  today we have global programs with many  cultural differences there's you know  thousands of em thousands of individuals  who are working in the space and they  all are dealing with different cultural  differences jurisdictional differences  institutional differences and so it's  very very difficult to get complete  buy-in and consensus on how to move  forward and this is just a an indication  of some of the guidelines and policies  that have been developed by various  groups internationally just to show you  the extent of diversity of groups and  individuals that are thinking about data  sharing norms the second issue has