 really compelling and we  need to protect individuals and so we  need to strengthen our privacy  protections and our security and and how  we're dealing with the sharing of  genomic data so whatever side you fall  on I think there's no denying that this  actually just complicates the issue a  lot more than what we knew in 1996 the  next two issues have to do with the  diversity of the data so back in 1996  with the Bermuda principles we're really  dealing primarily with human sequence  data and now I think there's a  recognition that we need layers of  genomic data as well as metabolomic  proteomics imaging environmental data as  we want to link it we don't only want  diversity of data we want to link it to  individuals and this idea that if you  have completely sort of anonymous if you  can you know you know we know you can't  anonymize DNA data but anonymize DNA  data  is really no longer that useful to us we  need to know who it came from we need to  have longitudinal data and we need to  have context and so that's a much more  complicated thing to try to manage so  again I bring us back to this idea of  building a medical information Commons  and this idea of having layers of data  that's linked to individuals was very  clearly set out by the National  Academies of science in 2011 in their  report so finally I think there's a  diversity of commercial interests that  didn't exist back in 1996 where the one  goal of the human genome project was to  sequence the human genome that was sort  of the overriding role and everybody was  on board that that's what they wanted to  accomplish now we have many many kinds  of companies with disparate business  models and very different motivations  for why they're in this space and so  this is a paper I'll just reference that  we published in 2014 in the journal  Nature Biotechnology and we looked at a  bunch of companies that were involved in  next-generation sequencing and we sort  of mapped what area along the sequencing  pipeline were they involved in it you  can see from the from the graphic here  these represent individual companies and  what they're doing all the way from you  know developing and selling consumables  and arrays just you know producing  sequencing instruments and supplying  those conducting the sequencing aligning  and annotate and annotating the  sequences that were being done  interpretation and reporting and data  storage and bundling and this is again  not an exhaustive list that these are  the kinds of things that these various  companies are engaged in and what  they're actually doing is going to  impact what their motivation is whether  they have access to data or whether they  can share data and what what impacts  sort of different legal regulatory  issues have on them as a company so I  think where we're at now is really  trying to figure out as a community how  do we incentivize data sharing and I  feel really strongly that although we  may agree that as a scientific community  it is a moral imperative  if we're going to offer whole genome and  whole exome sequencing to patients in a  clinical context that we have robust  enough databases to be able to give them  answers to what we find and so there may  be a moral imperative to share data  broadly speaking and we can we can argue  about that but let's let's just assume  that but even if it is a moral  imperative I don't think that's enough  it can't just be a moral imperative so  many people have pointed out that we  need a business imperative for change  and innovation based on the needs of our  customers and that the barriers to  sharing a really a lack of enabling  infrastructure data governance uniform  policies appropriately constrained  standards and economic incentives so we  need to be thinking about this not just  from an ethical moral perspective but  also from a business perspective so this  leads me back to our policy Delphi that  we did and rounds three and four of that  of that project we took sort of the  top-ranking policy challenges and then  we asked them what should we do about  this and so with regard to data sharing  we asked them what are potential  solutions for dealing with this issue of  data sharing and Indian companies having  proprietary databases and not sharing  data and so this is a little bit of a  difficult graph to read but you can see  here in the blue line shows sort of what  there was support for so you can see  here that down here um there was support  really the highest level of support was  for making data sharing and the  possibility of independent verification  a condition of approval or clearance  certification or accreditation so using  sort of our existing mechanisms like the  FDA or the CMS or cap accreditation to  basically say you have to share data and  making it a condition another another  option that there was some support for  was using positive economic incentives  so that payers reimburse more for tests  from laboratories that share data and  the one the one that received the less  the least amount of support was this  option of doing nothing and that you  know the idea that you can't compel data  disclosure in this space and people were  really opposed to that concept that we  do nothing and just let this happen so  now what we're doing in our group to  build on that project is we have another  grant that's called building the medical  information Commons and it's looking at  participant engagement and policy and we  have a fantastic Advisory Committee and  group of investigators &amp; Co  investigators that are really trying to  grapple with this issue of how can we  create a medical information comments  and we had a really exciting first  meeting back in March where we got at  all of these individuals who have  tremendous expertise in this area and  have been thinking about this area from  their own perspectives of being you know  somebody who works in a clinical lab or  somebody who's running a governmental  program or somebody who's in academia or  we had also people who were in the tech  industry who were doing this from from  that perspective and having them all sit  in a room together and be able to say  okay well this is what's important to us  but this is what's important to you guys  and can how can we work together to try  to think through the businesses  incentives as well as the ethical  imperative to share data and create this  medical information comments and so the  goal of our project is to engage expert  stakeholders to inform polities policy  decisions about effective governance for  data sharing while using deliberative  methods to obtain informed public input  to make sure that the values rights and  interests of individuals whose data  populate the information Commons are  represented so we're doing that in three  stages we're currently working on a  landscape analysis really trying to get  a handle on what are the different  initiatives that are out there in these  different spaces and how are they  sharing data what models are they using  and what are they currently doing and  then we're going to be interviewing  experts stakeholders including members  of our advisory panel but as well as as  other individuals to try to get a sense  of how well identified models of what's  currently going on address key policy  issues and to explore innovative ideas  for addressing unresolved issues and  then finally we're going to take sort of  a couple of different models that are  that are being developed in terms of how  we create this medical information  Commons to members of the public through  citizens panels and this is really an  in-depth way of exploring the values and  soliciting public in  about these key policy issues and the  ways to address innovative innovative  problems and so we'll be doing citizens  panels across the United States to try  to get this really really in-depth  informed public input on some of these  issues so we're really really excited  about this project this is some very  preliminary data based on the first 22  initiatives that we've done extensive  data collection on and this is from a  publicly available data on the internet  but you can see the kinds of data that  are being banked it's not just genetic  data anymore it's not just sequence data  anymore its data its proteins and  phenotypes images demographics other  kinds of information so this gets back  to the issue of linking data and you can  see the kind of data sharing models that  are being adopted so of the 22 that we  initially looked at about thirty two  percent of them have the centralized  model 60 point four percent used a  centralized hub and about five percent  are currently using a federated model  and there may be other models out there  that we need to explore as well that  that offer alternative types of  structures so I just want to kind of I'm  getting close to ending but I want to  point out what participants perspectives  are on this and what we know about that  from our own work so this is from a  study that was done actually several  years ago but we asked individuals who  are being recruited into genomic studies  and these were primarily patients who  were sick themselves or parents of  pediatric patients who had sick kids and  they were asked to never being asked to  participate in genomic studies have  their biological specimens stored and  used for genomic analysis and then to  have their data shared and we did a  randomized trial of different types of  consents that gave varying amounts of  control over the decision to share their  data and the the different options we  gave them is whether their data can be  shared in publicly accessible databases  or open access databases versus shared  in these controlled access databases  where you have to go through like a data  access committee to get the data or not  sure at all and then at the end of the  study we sort of told everybody we  debrief them and said here's what we did  here are all the options for you and how  would you like to have your data shared  so at the end of the study when we gave  everybody  all of the options the majority of our  participants agreed to share their data  either publicly which was fifty-three  percent of them or in controlled access  databases which was thirty-three percent  of them now their motivation for sharing  their data was really altruistic they  wanted to advance research and I have to  do a caveat here which is that you know  our participants although he has I think  335 participants it wasn't a small  number but these were all individuals  who were sick and they were highly  motivated to participate in research  they were all in a large academic  medical setting there was a high high  high level of trust in the institution  as well as in the physician that was  seeking their informed consent and so  this might not scale to other  populations and we need to be very  sensitive to variation in how people  feel about this and so for example we  had a much smaller pilot study that we  did with very disadvantaged poor  primarily african-american participants  who were HIV positive and we asked them  how they felt about sharing their data  and we had kind of a very very inverse  finding which was that most of them were  concerned about sharing their data and  they cared a lot more about their  privacy and that outweighed their desire  to advance research so we do need to be  sensitive to that there might be other  populations as well or individuals who  have particular feelings about this that  don't conform to the majority but for  our participants also we asked them how  important what it was to them to be  involved in the decision about sharing  their data and eighty-six percent felt  that it was very important for them to  be involved and we asked how they wanted  to be involved in most of them just  wanted to be asked and it wasn't because  they were terribly concerned about their  privacy it was really about feeling  respected as a participant in the  research and so they felt like that was  a really important aspect of respect for  persons so in our in our new study that  we're doing on building a medical  information Commons we're going to try  to take the this sort of survey  interview data that we have from actual  research participants and try to explore  the values of the public under  buying data sharing and so we're going  to do this public deliberation and we're  going to look at public views on several  of the ethical issues that come up as  well as try to get at the value tensions  that are at stake when we talk about  sharing data and building a medical  information commons and i just want to  end by pointing out that i think one of  the major big elephants in the room when  we talk about data sharing when we talk  about data repositories and medical  information commons is this question of  who owns your genetic data and this is a  photo I don't know how many of you can  see this well but it's a it's a  photograph I found on the Internet of an  elephant on President Obama's back and  the reason I include this picture of  President Obama is because a couple  months ago he held a conference in the  white house around the precision  medicine initiative and in the  conference he was sort of coded as  saying I would like to think that if  somebody does a test on me or my genes  that that's mine but that's not always  how we define these issues so this  really brings up the question of who  owns your genetic data and what do we  mean by ownership I think what lawyers  mean by ownership might be very very  different than what patients and  participants think of when they say it's  my data or I own the data and what  rights and obligations do those various  meanings give rise to and I think that's  one of the critical issues that we need  to address as we start moving towards  building a medical information comments  so i will end 