ills out the surveys and answers the  question so we're going to great lengths  to try to get both mom and dad where  there is a mom and dad to complete the  survey so that we have a better  understanding of how dads and men feel  about these issues as they relate to  their newborn children um the question  about what do you what do you do with  symptomatic health issues um I'm not one  hundred percent sure I understand that  question but you know I think when we  have individuals we really use genomic  sequencing in a couple different context  one is that you have an individual who  has a particular phenotype they're sick  and you're trying to get a diagnosis  you're trying to understand what's going  on with them and in some cases in very  few cases actually but you're trying to  sometimes we can actually figure out not  just what's going on with them but is  there something  can do to help improve their their  prognosis or their health care in other  cases you may have somebody like Hannah  who is currently asymptomatic but you  find that they have a genetic variant  that shows that they're an increased  risk for a particular disease or  disorder and for some of those they're  actionable so there might be things and  this is sort of the the public health  justification for what's included on  newborn screening panels is that you're  supposed to only be screening for things  that not only occur in childhood but  that are actionable so you can do  something to either prevent or treat the  disease early on but for some things we  don't know if they're actionable we  don't know what to do about them so for  this particular case there was nothing  clearly actionable that could be done  other than increased surveillance and  screening for early signs of the disease  so I hope that answered your question  your question it was an interesting  question is those are great answers  thank you next we have a question from  Korean who asked how can the rapid  evolution of genomic affect genetic  counseling so that is a fantastic  question I think one of the major  challenges that we have right now is  that we do not have enough genetic  counselors and even the genetic  counselors that we do have not all of  them have been trained and how to do  sort of what we call genomic counseling  right so they may be very comfortable  and familiar with how to counsel  families about single gene tests that  are being done but may not be as  familiar or comfortable with how to  counsel around genomic findings and so I  think a lot of the like we have a new  genetic counseling master's program  that's starting at Baylor College of  Medicine and I think that will focus a  lot on sort of genomic counseling and  training people and being comfortable  with genome-wide results and how to  interpret that in how to counsel  families and individuals on the basis of  those results but there's a slide that I  don't have in my slide deck but I  seen before that shows the number of new  postings for genetic counseling jobs  that are out there and and it's up here  and the number of genetic counselors  that are coming out of training programs  and it's like down here right so there's  a huge gap between the perceived need  for genetic counselors and the number of  genetic counselors that we have at our  disposal for for the field and so you  know this raises a couple of issues one  is are we going to increase the number  of genetic counselors and train more  people and I think we're trying to do  that and if this really scales based on  that first that first slide that I  showed with regard to how quickly we're  integrating genomic so I'm going to try  to pull it back up here if this really  scales at this level of being you know  we're doing a million now worldwide and  you know based on any of these we're  going to be doing a billion genomic  sequences eventually right if that  really scales at that level I think  we're going to have to come to grips  with the fact that all clinicians are  going to have to feel more comfortable  than they do now with helping to  interpret genomic sequencing findings  and helping to counsel patients and  their families and then really referring  for the most complicated cases so in the  med seek study we're actually studying  that the results are being given back by  primary care physicians and  cardiologists and they're receiving some  minimal training from our study team and  then kind of were auto-drivers with  their patients to really look at how do  they do when they're given a genome  report and they have to educate and  counsel their patients on what it means  for them and they're doing pretty well  actually there's there's they're not  they're not having a lot of examples of  misinformation or anything wrong and  we're watching for that okay XO  next we have a question from Tammy who  asked what what models allow patients to  have access to their medical records and  business impact the patient's  perspective on sharing of medical data  so that's another great question and  it's one that I think we're really  struggling with so the we had this  meeting I mentioned in March with a lot  of experts on who are dealing with data  sharing from a lot of different  perspectives and one of the key themes  that came up from that meeting was that  we really need to be participants  centric in how we do this and so it's  it's it's not going to work to ask  individuals to share their data and not  give them access to their own  information and so we need to do a much  better job at not only creating the  regulatory structure in all the  different countries for people to be  able to access their medical record and  their their genomic data and everything  else but to actually make it possible  for them to do that so you know we do  have laws in place that say you can  access your medical record we actually  have laws now in the United States and  say you can access your raw data and  your lab reports but most people find  that very difficult to accomplish and to  do and you know there's kind of a whole  bunch of bureaucracy they have to go to  in order to get access to the  information that they want so i think  that's that's an area that we really  need to work on and i do think it's sort  of a principle of reciprocity that if  we're going to ask people to contribute  to the medical information commons they  need to be able to get back at least  their own information from that same  comments  okay great um finally we have a question  from Sylvia and her question pertains to  ethics and data sharing from diseases or  from born vectors such as mosquitoes  which I don't know if you can comment on  that for us yeah so I'm not sure I'm the  right person to comment on that I'm  really sorry I'm not sure exactly what  the question is I think you know they're  there is a huge need to be thinking  about infectious diseases you know with  the Zika virus and and the other  infectious diseases that we're seeing  through mosquitoes and other pathogens  and thinking about how can we share data  related to that it's not an area that  I've done a tremendous amount of work in  but I think it's a really really  important area to be thinking about and  how do we link that with all of the  other medical information about  individuals is is going to be incredibly  important okay um next we have a  question from Kathleen and the question  is a little bit long so feel free to  read along with me Amy she asks a unique  RFID type extremely confidential label  be assigned to any data originating from  each individual patient and then she  says this could then be used to identify  any breach of confidentiality in the use  or accessibility of their data and  allowed for the individual patient to  monitor their data use ok yeah so much  I'm trying to look at this again I'm not  sure if I'm the best person to answer  this but I do think that there's ways to  identify breaches and confidentiality  and there are certain groups I would I  think the group out of Vanderbilt is  probably to my knowledge from those  forward thinking on this and how they're  tracking  access to data and looking at sort of  legitimate use and identifying breaches  in data access and they have a very  sophisticated sort of informatics way of  doing this that it's a one-way  filtration system I don't fully  understand it Brian Malin is kind of the  expert on this and for those who are  interested in this topic I'd recommend  that you reach out to him but I do think  there's ways to do this and I think the  issue that you bring up with regard to  focusing not on the front end which is  data access right so restricting who can  access the data putting it behind many  many firewalls etc but rather focusing  more on the back end of who is in an  unauthorized fashion accessing data  who's using data in harmful ways or  against you know data use agreements and  really having sort of strong punishments  or consequences for that type of misuse  I think that's where we need to be  focusing a little bit more as i  mentioned in europe the human tissue act  really sort of criminalizes the analysis  of DNA without consent that's one  approach on you know we can argue about  whether that's the right approach but at  least it focuses on sort of what are  people doing with the data if they have  access to it and not so much sort of how  are we storing the data and who's  contributing it and those sorts of  things I think you need to address it  from both sides but in the United States  at least I think we have been overly  focused on the front end and don't have  as as as strong protections for the back  end misuse  okay guess we have a question from Diane  and who says the issue of genetic  ownership varies across culture within  the United States some Native Americans  feel their genetic data reflects  extended kinship networks hence  ownership is a non-issue does your  research seek to address issues like  these or would citizen panels of a cross  cultural representation yeah so thank  you for that question that's another  excellent question I'm absolutely true  different people different cultures feel  very differently about issues of  ownership so our study and you know I'm  glad you brought up Native Americans  because I think that's a population in  the United States where we have really  sort of had an interesting relationship  with those with those groups with regard  to genetic research and the recent  havasu paid tribe case of Arizona where  there was a lot of controversy about the  use of genetic samples from the Hobby  soup a tribe really sort of highlighted  some of the cultural differences and and  and how we think about this and some of  the dangers of not paying attention to  that and being respectful of that so our  particular study is is focused on is not  focused on that we will ask those which  we try to get broad representation from  different you know demographic  backgrounds and our citizen panels and  we will ask about values around  ownership and so hopefully some of those  issues will come up but we are working  very closely with two individuals to  scholars who are those Native American  their Native Americans their  representatives of the Native American  communities and they're also scholars in  ethical legal and social implications of  genetics and genomics so Jesse bar dill  and on non of a garrison they're both  consultants on our project and we're  working with them to try to figure out  can we do some parallel research with  the Native American community to try to  see how we can sort of be respectful of  and be attentive to the particular  particular cultural values that exist in  those communities and  you know I don't want to say that all  Native American tribes have the same  culture around this either I think there  are a lot of differences in various  communities and we need to be very  sensitive to that okay i